
This is where you can build a simple Fortran/Python interface without
the burden of ACES II. Use simple Fortran codes like add.F substrat.F
etc.


Generating .pyf files:
python -m numpy.f2py add.F -m add -h add.pyf
python -m numpy.f2py check_strngs.F -m check_strngs -h check_strngs.pyf

If we want one pyf file that can use add and check_strgs edit the 
add.pyf file and embed the check_strngs to it as follows. 
--------------------------------------------------------------------
python module add ! in
    interface  ! in :add
        subroutine add(a,b,n) ! in :add:add.F
            double precision dimension(n) :: a
            double precision dimension(n),depend(n) :: b
            integer*8, optional,check(shape(a, 0) == n),depend(a) :: n=shape(a, 0)
            double precision dimension(10) :: a_1
            logical :: set_it
            common /internal/ a_1,set_it
        end subroutine add
    end interface

!python module check_strngs ! in
    interface  ! in :check_strngs
        subroutine check_strngs(a,b,d) ! in :check_strings:check_strngs.F
            character*5 :: a
            character*9 :: b
            character*16 intent(inout) :: d
        end subroutine check_strngs
    end interface
!end python module check_strngs

end python module add

Note that it is not necessary to combine these two. One can use two independent
.pyf files like

!python module check_strngs ! in
    interface  ! in :check_strngs
        subroutine check_strngs(a,b,d) ! in :check_strings:check_strngs.F
            character*5 :: a
            character*9 :: b
            character*16 intent(inout) :: d
        end subroutine check_strngs
    end interface
!end python module check_strngs

end python module add


---------------------------------------------------------------------------

This allows us to have a master pyf file that contains python interfaces to 
all other Fortran attributes. This is important when we are delaing with 
large Fortran pakages like ACES II with multiple modules (some are libraries 
and the others are binaries and/or libraries). 

Then do the final step of building the add.cpython-310-x86_64-linux-gnu.so file.
We can rename it add.so if you like. What is important is the your LD_LIBRARY_PATH
knows the locations of all shared object file (or libraries)

This now uses numpy utlity called numpy.distutils. There are multiple ways to 
the python shared object. MOre details can be found in  numpy documenation.

https://numpy.org/doc/stable/f2py/buildtools

The distutil command is 

python -m numpy.f2py --fcompiler=intelem -c -m  add add.pyf add.F substract.so check_strngs.so

The shared object check_strngs was  generated by
ifort -traceback -shared -fPIC -O2 -Zp8 -zero -i8 -o check_strngs.so check_strngs.F

Any valid Fortran compiler options work. When there are multiple object file, one
can create a shared object archive (similar to .a  now .so)

Testing
Python test.py check the combined python utility add.
Python check.py check check_strngs as a seperate utility

P.S.: compile_add and compile_check_strngs are simple shell scrpits for the final 
step 





